WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:05.920
Did you ever wonder if certain foods
have a direct effect on your health?

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.000
Well, I’m here to
solve that mystery

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.360
since you are
the foods you eat.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.440
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.320
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.960
We know it’s possible to
cut down our salt intake.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.640
So, what are three practical
steps we can take?

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.000
The two most prominent
dietary risks

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.840
for death and disability
in the world

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:33.240
are not eating enough fruit
and eating too much salt.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.400
Too little fruit kills nearly
five million people every year,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.960
and too much salt
kills four million.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:40.320
There are three things
we can do

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.120
to lower our
salt intake.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.760
First, don’t add salt
at the table.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.320
A third of us add
salt to our food

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.640
before even tasting it!

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.720
Number two: Stop adding
salt when cooking.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.280
At first the food
will taste bland.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.040
Two to four weeks later, however,

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.480
as the sensitivity of
the salt taste receptors

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.280
in the mouth become
more sensitive

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.760
to the taste of salt in
the usual concentrations.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.120
Believe it or not
but after two weeks,

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.440
you may actually
prefer the taste

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:13.760
of food with less salt.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.560
Some of the flavorings you can use instead
in the meanwhile instead of salt

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:21.120
include using more pepper,
onion, garlic, tomato,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.280
sweet peppers, basil, parsley,
thyme, celery, lime, chilli,

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:30.200
nettle, rosemary, smoke flavor,
curry, coriander, and lemon.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.200
Even if you did add salt, though,

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.520
it’s probably better
than eating out,

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.440
where even at non-fast
food restaurants,

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:38.280
they tend to
pile it on.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:43.320
And finally, avoid processed
foods that have salt added.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.240
In most countries only
about half of sodium intake

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.320
comes from processed foods,

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.600
so there’s more personal
responsibility,

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:50.720
but in the U.S.,

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.960
even if we completely
stopped adding salt

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.360
in the kitchen and
dining room,

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.040
it would only bring down salt
intake a small fraction.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.920
This has led public
health commentators

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.080
to note how challenging
it is then for everyone

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.920
to reduce their
salt intake

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.320
since so much of our sodium intake
is out of our control.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.400
But is it?

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.920
We don’t have to buy all
those processed foods.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.240
We can choose not to turn
over our family’s health

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.280
to food corporations
that may not have

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.720
our best interests
at heart.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.440
If we do buy processed foods
there are two tricks we can use

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.760
try to only buy foods
with fewer miligrams of sodium

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:31.360
on the label than there
are grams in the serving size.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.320
So if it’s a 100 gram
serving size,

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:36.840
it should have less than
100mg of sodium.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.920
Or, you can shoot for
fewer milligrams of sodium

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.480
than there are calories.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.920
For example here the
sodium is 720;

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.080
calories are 260.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:52.920
720 is greater than 260,
so this has too much sodium.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:53.680
That’s a trick I learned

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.760
from one of my favorite
dieticians of all time,

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.120
Jeff Novick.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:58.840
The reason it works is
because most people get

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.400
about 2,200 calories a day,

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.560
so if everything you ate
had more calories than sodium

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:06.720
you’d at least get under
2,300 milligrams of sodium

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.240
upper limit for healthy
people under age 50.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.280
Of course, the healthiest foods
have no labels at all.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.000
We should also try to buy as
much fresh food as possible,

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.480
as it is almost impossible
to come up with a diet

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:22.240
consisting of unprocessed
natural foodstuffs

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.160
that exceeds the strict
American Heart Association guidelines

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:28.120
for sodium reduction.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.080
In our next story,

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:33.480
we look at the ways in which salt
may drive autoimmune diseases

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.800
such as multiple
sclerosis, psoriasis,

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.520
type 1 diabetes,
Sjögren’s syndrome,

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.320
asthma, and
rheumatoid arthritis.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:46.640
Millions suffer from asthma
attacks triggered by exercise.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.000
Within 5 minutes of
starting exercising,

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.560
people can get short of breath,
start coughing and wheezing,

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.760
such that lung function
significantly drops.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.720
But, on a high-salt diet,
the attack is even worse;

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:02.160
whereas, on a
low-salt diet,

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:05.840
there’s hardly a significant
drop in function at all.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.040
To figure out why, researchers
had them all cough up

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.160
sputum from their lungs,
and those on the high-salt diet

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:17.000
ended up with triple
the inflammatory cells

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.080
and up to double the concentration
of inflammatory mediators.

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:22.880
But why?

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.080
What does salt intake have
to do with inflammation?

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.880
We didn’t know...

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.160
until now.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:35.800
The “Western diet,” high in
saturated fat and salt,

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.600
has long been postulated
as one potential cause

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:39.880
for the increasing incidence

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:43.480
of autoimmune diseases
in developed countries.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:46.840
The rapidly increasing incidence
of autoimmune diseases

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:50.120
may be due to an over-
activation of immune cells

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:53.200
called helper 17 cells.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:57.360
The development of multiple sclerosis,
psoriasis, type I diabetes,

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:00.480
Sjögren’s syndrome, asthma,
and rheumatoid arthritis

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:05.920
has all been shown to involve
this Th17-driven inflammation,

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:09.760
and one trigger for the
activation of those Th17 cells

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:14.240
may be elevated levels of
salt in our bloodstream.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.000
The sodium content of processed
foods and ‘fast food’

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.280
can be more than
100 times higher

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.080
in comparison to
similar homemade meals.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:29.160
And, sodium chloride, salt, appears
to drive autoimmune disease

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:33.640
by the induction of these
disease-causing Th17 cells.

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:37.760
Turns out, there’s a salt-
sensing enzyme responsible

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:41.720
for triggering the formation
of these Th17 cells.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:44.400
Organ damage caused
by high-salt diets

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.760
may also activate another type
of inflammatory immune cell.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:52.280
A high-salt diet can
overwork the kidneys,

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.440
starving them of oxygen,
triggering inflammation.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.480
The more salt they
gave people,

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:03.120
the more activation of
inflammatory monocyte cells,

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:08.400
associated with high-salt intake
induced kidney oxygen deficiency,

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:11.400
but this study only
lasted two weeks.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:13.920
What about long term?

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.560
One of the difficulties in
doing sodium experiments

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.760
is that it’s hard to
get free-living folks

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:21.120
to maintain a
specific salt intake.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:24.040
You can do what are called
metabolic ward studies,

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.960
where you essentially lock
people in a hospital ward

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:28.080
for a few days and control
their food intake,

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:31.840
but you can’t do that
long term, unless...

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:35.400
you can lock people
in a space capsule.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:40.800
Mars520 was a 520-day
space flight simulation

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:44.640
to see how people might do
on the way to Mars and back.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:47.880
What they found was that
those on a high-salt diet

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:51.200
displayed a markedly higher
number of monocytes,

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:54.520
which are a type of immune
cell you often see increased

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:58.880
in settings of chronic inflammation
and autoimmune disorders.

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:01.200
This may reveal one
of the consequences

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.480
of excess salt consumption
in our everyday lives,

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:07.920
since that so-called high-salt
intake may actually

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:11.160
just be the average
salt intake.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:13.520
Furthermore, there was
an increase in the levels

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:15.600
of pro-inflammatory mediators

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:19.000
and a decrease in the level of
anti-inflammatory mediators,

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.320
suggesting that high-salt diets
have a potential to bring about

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.880
an excessive immune response,
which may damage the immune balance

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.680
and result in either
difficulties

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:30.120
in getting rid of
inflammation or

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:34.240
even an increased risk
of autoimmune disease.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:37.840
What if you already have
an autoimmune disease?

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:42.200
Sodium intake is associated with
increased disease activity

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:43.960
in multiple sclerosis.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:47.160
If you follow MS
patients for a few years,

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:49.640
those eating more salt

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:52.880
had 3 to 4 times the
exacerbation rate;

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:55.440
three times more
likely to develop

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:58.640
new MS lesions
in their brains,

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:02.880
and on average, had 8
more brain lesions,

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:04.560
14 in their brain
compared to

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:07.480
6 in the low-salt group.

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:09.960
So, the next step is
to try treating patients

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.040
with salt reduction and
see if they get better.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:15.280
But since reducing
our salt intake

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:18.200
is a healthy thing
to do anyway,

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:21.880
I don’t see why anyone
should have to wait.

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:26.080
Did you know that salt can impair
artery function within 30 minutes

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:30.560
by suppressing a key
antioxidant enzyme in our body?

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:32.120
Here’s the story.

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.560
If you put people
on a low salt diet,

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:37.720
meaning only getting twice as
much sodium as they need,

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:39.400
as opposed to a
usual salt diet

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.240
where they’re getting
five times more,

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:43.280
you get a significant
improvement

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:45.760
in artery function.

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:49.440
Lower salt,
better arterial function;

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:51.080
suggesting heart
protective effects

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:53.640
beyond just blood
pressure reduction.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.000
Now this was after dropping
people’s salt intake

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.680
by about a teaspoon
a day for two weeks.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.440
What if you only
dropped salt intake

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:03.200
by like a half
teaspoon a day?

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:06.480
You still get a significant improvement
in artery function,

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.360
and it happens within
just two days

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.680
of reducing one’s
salt intake.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:14.520
Or even after
a single meal.

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.240
A high salt meal,
which is to say

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:18.040
just a typical amount
of salt consumed

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:20.160
in a commonly
eaten meal,

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:23.120
can significantly suppress
artery function

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:25.785
within 30 minutes.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.280
Now is this in addition
to the spike

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:29.520
in blood pressure
from salt

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:32.400
or because of the spike
in blood pressure?

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.480
If you take people with
normal blood pressure

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:36.120
and give them a
bowl of soup

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:39.400
containing how much salt
a regular meal might contain,

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:40.840
their blood pressure
goes up

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:42.360
over the next
three hours

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:43.560
compared to the
same soup

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:45.560
with no added salt.

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:47.160
Now this doesn’t happen
to everyone;

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:49.120
this is just the
average response.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:51.080
Some people are resistant
to the effects

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:53.120
of salt on their
blood pressure.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:54.640
So, what if
you repeated

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:57.525
the artery function
experiment on them?

00:09:57.585 --> 00:09:58.400
Even in people

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:01.120
whose blood pressure is
unresponsive to salt intake,

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:04.280
they still suffer
significant suppression

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:05.880
of their artery
function.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:08.680
So even independent of
any effects on blood pressure,

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:10.480
salt hurts our arteries

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.440
and that harm begins
within minutes

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.040
of it going into
our mouth

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:16.920
for our major
arteries

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:20.200
and even our itty
bitty blood vessels.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:22.760
Using something called
laser Doppler flowmetry,

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:24.200
you can measure
blood flow

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:26.541
in the tiny vessels
in our skin.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:28.480
Now to get the blood
vessels to open up

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:30.720
they warmed
up the skin.

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:32.440
The reason we
may turn pink

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:33.840
when we get into
a hot bath

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:37.200
is that the blood vessels in
our skin are opening up.

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:38.720
And that’s
what happens.

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:41.320
Big increase in blood flow
with the warming.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:43.160
But that’s on the
low salt diet.

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:44.400
A high salt diet,

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:45.800
starts out the same
at the beginning,

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:47.720
but after the
same heating,

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:50.600
there’s significantly
less blood flow.

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:52.800
The arteries just don't
seem to open up as well

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:55.480
on a high salt diet,
unless...

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:59.320
you inject vitamin C
into their skin.

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:01.360
That seems to reverse
the salt-induced

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:04.280
suppression of blood
vessel function.

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:07.040
So if an antioxidant
reverses the salt effect,

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:10.280
then the way salt may be
damaging our artery function

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:11.720
is through
oxidative stress,

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:14.560
the formation of free radicals
in our blood stream.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:16.240
But how?

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:17.920
There’s an enzyme
in our body

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:23.560
that can detoxify a million
free radicals per second,

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:26.040
24 hours a day,
7 days a week.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:27.960
But compared to
a low salt diet,

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:29.760
if we consume a
normal salt diet,

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:32.400
we suppress the
activity of this

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:35.880
detoxifying powerhouse
of an enzyme.

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:38.729
That may be
help explain why

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:41.520
with our antioxidant enzymes
crippled by the salt

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.280
all the excess
free radicals

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:44.800
may be crippling
our arteries.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:47.240
But mop up those extra
free radicals up

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:50.000
by infusing vitamin C
into the bloodstream,

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:52.680
and artery function
returns to normal.

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:54.280
Whereas on a
low salt diet,

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:57.600
if you drip vitamin C
into people’s veins,

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:58.760
nothing happens,

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:00.320
because our antioxidant
enzymes

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:02.360
are already taking care
of business

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:05.000
and haven’t been shackled
by the sodium

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:07.280
of a normal-salt diet.

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:08.120
Whereas potassium,

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:10.320
concentrated in fruits
and vegetables,

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:12.360
soften the cells
that line our arteries

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:14.400
and increase the
release of nitric oxide

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:16.960
that allows our
arteries to relax,

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:19.760
sodium in our blood
stiffens the cells

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:22.200
lining our arteries
within minutes,

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:25.360
and reduces nitric
oxide release.

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:31.400
The more salt, the less
nitric oxide is produced.

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:34.440
One salty meal, and not only
does our blood pressure go up

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:36.760
but our arteries
literally stiffen.

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:38.480
That’s why we
could figure out

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:40.760
that too much salt
was bad for us

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:43.200
4,000 years ago.

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:44.760
We don’t need a
double blind trial,

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:47.400
maybe we don’t need to follow
people out for a decade.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:49.040
You may just have
to feed someone

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:54.089
a bag of potato chips
and take their pulse.

00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:56.960
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:00.400
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:03.120
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:07.320
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:11.240
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:14.720
please go to the Nutrition Facts
Podcast landing page.

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:16.960
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:21.080
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite for each of these topics.

00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:23.640
My latest book, “How Not to Age”
is out now,

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:27.360
which premiered at #2 on the
New York Times Best Sellers list.

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:29.640
Check it out at your
local public library.

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:32.000
Of course, all the proceeds
I receive of the sales

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:35.760
of all my books goes
directly to charity.

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:40.440
NutritionFacts.org is itself a nonprofit,
science-based public service,

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:42.280
where you can sign up
for free daily updates

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:46.320
on the latest in nutrition research
via bite-sized videos and articles.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:48.640
Everything on the
website is free.

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:49.600
There’s no ads,

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:51.320
no corporate sponsorships,
no kickbacks.

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:54.560
It’s strictly non-commercial.
I’m not selling anything.

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:57.600
I just put it up as a public service,
as a labor of love,

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.000
as a tribute to
my grandmother,

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.077
whose own life was saved
with evidence-based nutrition.

